1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,601
Ow! It bit me!

2
00:00:01,921 --> 00:00:02,595
Boston.

3
00:00:02,595 --> 00:00:06,768
The small hours of the 2nd of September, 1859.

4
00:00:06,933 --> 00:00:09,520
Your telegraph is throwing sparks.

5
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:13,309
And outside the window, the sky has turned the colour of blood.

6
00:00:13,629 --> 00:00:15,481
Fire! Half the city's on fire!

7
00:00:15,667 --> 00:00:18,430
But there is no smoke. No flames.

8
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:21,993
The whole sky is glowing, from one horizon to the other.

9
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:26,855
Before morning, your wire will spit fire, shock the men who touch it,

10
00:00:26,855 --> 00:00:29,545
and then do something nobody thought possible.

11
00:00:29,865 --> 00:00:33,355
It will carry messages with the battery unplugged.

12
00:00:33,355 --> 00:00:34,935
Powered by the sky.

13
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:42,380
You are Ned Barlow, 17 years old,

14
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:46,307
the junior night operator at a telegraph office in Boston.

15
00:00:46,627 --> 00:00:48,290
Ned is invented.

16
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:53,505
But almost everything that happens to him tonight happened to real operators,

17
00:00:53,505 --> 00:00:54,822
all over the world.

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,670
In 1859, the telegraph is the fastest thing on Earth.

19
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:05,510
News that once took days on horseback now crosses hundreds of miles in minutes,

20
00:01:05,510 --> 00:01:09,637
as pulses of electric current on a single iron wire.

21
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:15,665
A row of glass jars, a battery of acid and metal, pushes current down the line.

22
00:01:15,665 --> 00:01:16,430
You tap the key.

23
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:18,535
Far away, a sounder clicks.

24
00:01:18,535 --> 00:01:20,074
Dots, and dashes.

25
00:01:20,267 --> 00:01:23,080
A good operator doesn't even need to look.

26
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:27,222
You read the clicks by ear, the way other people hear words.

27
00:01:27,542 --> 00:01:29,934
Barlow can hear a typo from across the room.

28
00:01:30,254 --> 00:01:31,465
Only yours, sir.

29
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:34,822
Your boss is the night manager, Mister Pike.

30
00:01:35,142 --> 00:01:37,162
Barlow! The Portland line. Anything?

31
00:01:37,482 --> 00:01:38,751
Just crackling, sir.

32
00:01:39,071 --> 00:01:40,250
Well, make it stop.

33
00:01:40,433 --> 00:01:42,355
It has been a strange week.

34
00:01:42,355 --> 00:01:47,140
Four nights ago, the northern lights came much further south than usual,

35
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:51,313
and telegraph lines across the country went haywire for hours.

36
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:53,583
Then things calmed down.

37
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,855
Nothing ever happens on the night wire.

38
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:01,480
But the trouble had started the morning before, 3,000 miles away,

39
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:05,505
at a private observatory in Redhill, just south of London.

40
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:08,785
Richard Carrington is an amateur astronomer,

41
00:02:08,785 --> 00:02:11,865
and every clear morning he draws sunspots.

42
00:02:11,865 --> 00:02:14,285
He never looks at the sun directly.

43
00:02:14,285 --> 00:02:17,766
His telescope throws its image onto a white screen.

44
00:02:17,933 --> 00:02:20,985
Just before noon on the 1st of September,

45
00:02:20,985 --> 00:02:25,581
two beads of blinding white light burst out of a group of sunspots.

46
00:02:26,567 --> 00:02:29,850
He runs off to fetch someone to witness it.

47
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:31,465
He is back within 60 seconds.

48
00:02:31,465 --> 00:02:33,920
By then, it is already fading.

49
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:36,400
In about 5 minutes, it is gone.

50
00:02:36,567 --> 00:02:41,925
Across London, another observer, Richard Hodgson, saw the same flash.

51
00:02:41,925 --> 00:02:46,559
And at the Kew Observatory, the magnetic instruments gave a sudden jump.

52
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:51,640
Both men wrote up what they had seen for the Royal Astronomical Society.

53
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:54,750
Their reports were printed side by side.

54
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:58,396
It was the first solar flare anyone had ever recorded.

55
00:02:58,567 --> 00:03:04,530
Nobody knew it yet, but the sun had thrown a vast cloud of charged gas into space,

56
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:05,820
straight at the Earth.

57
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:10,500
A cloud like that usually takes days to arrive.

58
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:15,973
This one crossed 150 million kilometres in about 17½ hours.

59
00:03:16,167 --> 00:03:19,229
And late that night, in Boston, it arrives.

60
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:21,011
Fire! Somebody ring the bells!

61
00:03:21,331 --> 00:03:25,745
Many Bostonians first thought the red sky was a great fire.

62
00:03:25,745 --> 00:03:31,394
Then the red turned to green, and spires of light shot up toward the top of the sky.

63
00:03:31,567 --> 00:03:36,589
People pour into the streets in their nightclothes, pointing, whispering, praying.

64
00:03:36,767 --> 00:03:38,354
Ned, is it the end of the world?

65
00:03:38,674 --> 00:03:40,269
I don't think so, Mrs Kane.

66
00:03:40,669 --> 00:03:41,733
Probably not.

67
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:43,922
Barlow. I can read the shipping news.

68
00:03:44,242 --> 00:03:47,395
The light is so strong that in the northeast,

69
00:03:47,395 --> 00:03:50,365
people said you could read a newspaper by it.

70
00:03:50,365 --> 00:03:51,778
In the middle of the night.

71
00:03:51,967 --> 00:03:57,039
Far to the west, in the Rocky Mountains, gold miners woke up in the glow.

72
00:03:57,359 --> 00:03:58,582
Up, boys! It's morning!

73
00:03:58,902 --> 00:04:00,971
And they started cooking breakfast.

74
00:04:01,167 --> 00:04:05,640
In South Carolina, bricklayers reportedly got up and went to work,

75
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:08,330
until they realised what time it was.

76
00:04:08,330 --> 00:04:12,077
In Virginia, birds started singing at one in the morning.

77
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:16,850
The lights were seen as far south as Cuba, Jamaica and Hawaii.

78
00:04:16,850 --> 00:04:20,450
There were even reports from Colombia, close to the equator.

79
00:04:20,633 --> 00:04:22,550
But you don't get to watch for long.

80
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:27,424
Inside, your sounder is clattering all by itself, spelling nonsense.

81
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:29,100
It's sending by itself!

82
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:30,648
There's nobody on the other end!

83
00:04:30,833 --> 00:04:33,220
Sparks jump from the instruments.

84
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:34,815
At some stations,

85
00:04:34,815 --> 00:04:39,183
the chemically treated paper that records the messages caught fire.

86
00:04:39,367 --> 00:04:41,196
Mister Pike! The line's alive!

87
00:04:41,516 --> 00:04:42,386
Don't touch it!

88
00:04:42,886 --> 00:04:43,673
Too late.

89
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:44,940
In Washington,

90
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:48,255
an operator named Frederick Royce was badly shocked

91
00:04:48,255 --> 00:04:51,285
when his forehead brushed a ground wire.

92
00:04:51,285 --> 00:04:55,903
A witness said an arc of fire jumped from his head to the equipment.

93
00:04:56,100 --> 00:05:01,014
In Pittsburgh, the manager reported streams of fire pouring from the circuits.

94
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:05,370
Across North America and Europe, telegraph lines failed.

95
00:05:05,370 --> 00:05:09,517
For hours, the fastest network on Earth was close to useless.

96
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:16,290
What nobody in 1859 can know: the storm is shaking the Earth's magnetic field,

97
00:05:16,290 --> 00:05:19,827
and that drives electric currents into long wires.

98
00:05:20,147 --> 00:05:23,396
Your line is pulling power straight out of the sky.

99
00:05:23,567 --> 00:05:25,820
By the morning of the 2nd of September,

100
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:30,205
the Boston office still cannot send or receive properly.

101
00:05:30,205 --> 00:05:33,893
The current on the line keeps swelling and fading on its own.

102
00:05:34,067 --> 00:05:35,902
Sir, what if we take the battery off?

103
00:05:36,222 --> 00:05:37,537
Then nothing will work.

104
00:05:37,857 --> 00:05:42,880
Here is what the operators on the line between Boston and Portland, Maine,

105
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:44,755
actually did that morning.

106
00:05:44,755 --> 00:05:48,538
These are their words, as a Boston newspaper printed them.

107
00:05:48,733 --> 00:05:52,042
Please cut off your battery entirely from the line for 15 minutes.

108
00:05:52,362 --> 00:05:54,738
Will do so. It is now disconnected.

109
00:05:54,933 --> 00:05:58,355
Mine is disconnected, and we are working with the auroral current.

110
00:05:58,355 --> 00:05:59,831
How do you receive my writing?

111
00:06:00,151 --> 00:06:01,935
Better than with our batteries on.

112
00:06:01,935 --> 00:06:03,861
Current comes and goes gradually.

113
00:06:04,633 --> 00:06:05,611
Well. I'll be.

114
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:09,982
Suppose we work without batteries while we are affected by this trouble.

115
00:06:10,302 --> 00:06:12,585
Very well. Shall I go ahead with business?

116
00:06:12,905 --> 00:06:14,060
Yes. Go ahead.

117
00:06:14,233 --> 00:06:16,965
And they did. For about two hours,

118
00:06:16,965 --> 00:06:21,382
Boston and Portland traded messages powered by nothing but the storm.

119
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:22,560
The next day,

120
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:25,275
some lines were still being worked for a

121
00:06:25,275 --> 00:06:28,331
while by what people called atmospheric current alone.

122
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:28,900
Barlow.

123
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:31,142
Not a word to head office that we unplugged it.

124
00:06:31,462 --> 00:06:32,277
No, sir.

125
00:06:32,467 --> 00:06:37,085
Carrington suspected that the flash he saw and the storm were connected.

126
00:06:37,085 --> 00:06:38,565
But he was careful.

127
00:06:38,565 --> 00:06:41,600
One swallow, he wrote, does not make a summer.

128
00:06:41,767 --> 00:06:47,655
In America, the Yale professor Elias Loomis gathered reports from around the world,

129
00:06:47,655 --> 00:06:51,875
and published them in nine articles over the next three years.

130
00:06:52,067 --> 00:06:54,920
Today it is called the Carrington Event:

131
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:59,241
the most intense geomagnetic storm in recorded history.

132
00:06:59,433 --> 00:07:03,030
What you saw that night was the storm hitting the air itself.

133
00:07:03,030 --> 00:07:07,890
Charged particles slam into the upper atmosphere, and oxygen glows:

134
00:07:07,890 --> 00:07:10,855
green lower down, deep red higher up.

135
00:07:10,855 --> 00:07:14,885
In a storm this strong, the glow spreads far from the poles.

136
00:07:15,067 --> 00:07:21,185
In 1859, the worst it could do was shock a few operators and burn some paper.

137
00:07:21,185 --> 00:07:24,305
Today we run almost everything on wires.

138
00:07:24,305 --> 00:07:27,730
Power grids. Satellites. Navigation.

139
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:28,415
The internet.

140
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:34,150
In July 2012, the sun threw out a storm thought to be of similar strength.

141
00:07:34,150 --> 00:07:36,939
It missed the Earth by about 9 days.

142
00:07:37,133 --> 00:07:40,730
And smaller storms have already done real damage.

143
00:07:40,730 --> 00:07:46,620
In March 1989, one knocked out the power grid of Quebec, in Canada,

144
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:50,922
leaving about 6 million people in the dark for around 9 hours.

145
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:58,075
A 2013 study for the insurer Lloyd's of London estimated that a storm like

146
00:07:58,075 --> 00:08:05,709
1859's could cost the United States alone between $600 billion and $2.6 trillion.

147
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:07,050
And in May 2024,

148
00:08:07,050 --> 00:08:12,297
a far weaker storm brought the northern lights to skies far to the south,

149
00:08:12,297 --> 00:08:15,029
where most people had never seen them.

150
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:17,326
And you? Your shift finally ends.

151
00:08:17,326 --> 00:08:20,548
You walk home through streets still full of people

152
00:08:20,548 --> 00:08:23,383
talking about the night the sky caught fire.

153
00:08:23,567 --> 00:08:24,885
So, tell me.

154
00:08:24,885 --> 00:08:30,098
If the sky turned red tonight, and every screen went dead, what would you do first?

155
00:08:30,418 --> 00:08:31,668
Unplug the battery.

156
00:08:31,988 --> 00:08:32,834
Make coffee.

